  "notes": "Columbia soloist on Rich Harrison's go-go/breakbeat R&B — the research calls it \"the most percussion-forward artist lane here\", 2002–2007. Tempo is \"90–115 straight (backbeat 2&4, but the go-go feel puts accents all over the 16ths)\", mode 100, the sourced reading. The drum deltas are the loudest in this section and every one is quoted: a flipped drum-and-guitar breakdown \"with **added bongo, cowbell and ride cymbal** — a go-go second-line feel with cascading, near-continuous 16th and 32nd drum activity and no quantised hat pattern\". So the kick runs four to seven hits a bar at 0.7 syncopation, the snare carries ghosts on all eight sixteenth off-slots at a 0.7 probability, the hat lane is non-continuous (there is no quantised pattern) but at 0.85 fill density with a 32nd-led roll vocabulary at 0.7 per bar, the perc bed is five lanes at five to nine hits a bar and −2 dB, `quantizeStrength` is 0.56 and fills come every two bars. Harmony is \"one- or two-chord vamps under a chant-and-response topline\", modal minor inherited from the sampled funk source. \"Amerie's rapid, clipped, high-register delivery functions as another percussion layer. Note density high but interval range narrow\" is a 6–14 melody density — the highest in the section — with a 0.88 step share, staccato articulation and `call_response` structure. \"No 808 — the source break's guitar and bass carry the low-mid; label pressure to add instruments was refused\" is why there is no `bass808` block and the bass walks at `followRootsProb` 0.6. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ Every velocity, density and jitter number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"modal minor inherited from the sampled funk source [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry gives the one-chord and the two-chord bed equal billing — \"one- or two-chord vamps under a chant-and-response topline\" — and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\"), so a `vamp` weight of 6-of-8 made the two-chord half of that sentence almost unreachable. `vamp` keeps the top weight; the two-chord reading is now reachable at both rates. ⚠ The split between those two rates is **INFERRED** — the entry names the device, not its rate.",
